#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Mechanical checks before the approver, so it only has to judge.

  .factory/precheck <job-id> [<round>]
  .factory/precheck --validate <job-id> [<round>]
                                        the full validation, once per round: every Validate command
                                        of repo.md through `heavy --cache --background --priority high` in the job
                                        worktree; writes round-N/validate.md (first line
                                        `VALIDATE: PASS|FAIL|NONE`); still running after ~9 minutes:
                                        prints `VALIDATE: PENDING` and exits 124 (run it again).
                                        With `validate.mode: ci` (config.json, `pr` delivery) it runs
                                        nothing locally: it pushes the job branch, opens the PR/MR when
                                        `validate.open_pr` (always a draft until delivery; an open ready
                                        one is made a draft again), and waits for that commit's pipeline on the
                                        forge (through .factory/ci); `VALIDATE: ERROR <why>` when the
                                        push, the PR/MR or the pipeline cannot be had (a human looks).
                                        A pipeline that failed only on infrastructure (OOMKilled, exit
                                        137, runner/scheduler, registry push after green tests) is
                                        retried once (`CI: INFRA (<reason>), retried`); failing again
                                        it is `VALIDATE: INFRA <reason>`: no rework round
  .factory/precheck --pr-body <job-id>  write <job>/pr-body.md for delivery: the job's PR text (`## PR` of
                                        decision.md or build.md; title = its first line) plus one
                                        `<issues.link> #N` line for every issue of the job it does not
                                        close yet (a bundle: the bundle issue and every member); an issue
                                        marked partial (`Refs #N (left: <what>)`) is kept open; the footer
  .factory/precheck --closes <job-id> [<file>]
                                        check a PR/MR body: `CLOSES: OK`, or `CLOSES: MISSING #a, #b` (exit 1)
  .factory/precheck --hold <job-id>     only list the files of the job's diff that sit in a class
                                        CI alone does not vouch for (`<class>: <file>` per line);
                                        .factory/job uses it to lift a `light` job to `full`

Reads .factory/jobs/<job-id>/ (job.json, spec.md, round-N/*) and the job branch's diff
against its base, writes <job>/precheck.md and prints it. First line is
`PRECHECK: CLEAN` or `PRECHECK: ISSUES`; exit 0 either way (the orchestrator reads the line).
For a `"mode": "docs"` job there are no reviews: instead build.md must contain a `## PR`
section and every changed file must be a documentation path (`limits.docs_globs` in
config.json, default below); if that is the only problem the first line is
`PRECHECK: NOT-DOCS` so the orchestrator can fall back to the light review.

Checks (each failing one is listed under Issues):
  - round-N/build.md exists, and spec.md too (not in docs mode: there the feature text is the spec)
  - round-N/validate.md (written by --validate) does not say `VALIDATE: FAIL`
  - the latest review of every reviewer (from round 2 on only some re-run) starts with `VERDICT: PASS`;
    `VERDICT: PASS (unverified: tests did not run: <why>)` is an issue until decision.md has
    `VERIFIED: <reviewer>: <command → result>` (the approver ran those tests; then a note)
  - audit table: no row marked ❌ or ⏭️ (a ⚠️ row is listed as a note, not an issue; a row whose
    only ⚠️ marks are `⚠️ ENV: <cause with the measured load, or a network cause>` (environment-only)
    or `⚠️ TEST: <why the hidden acceptance test is wrong> · criterion verified by <evidence>`
    (test-only): noted, but no deviation for the auto-merge gate); a ⚠️ or ⏭️ row that the approver
    accepts in decision.md (`ACCEPTED: audit row <n>: <reason>`) is confirmed: a note, no deviation,
    and --pr-body quotes the line (.factory/job re-runs the precheck at delivery for it); never a ❌ row
  - the diff touches nothing under .factory/ or .claude/
  - no secret-looking files (.env, keys, certificates, credentials, *.sops.* decrypted copies)
  - no secret-looking content in added lines (tokens, private keys); only the file and kind are shown
  - the diff is not huge (> 40 files or > 1500 changed lines) and has no binary or > 1 MB file
  - a job from issues whose PR text exists already (docs: build.md; a re-run after the approver:
    decision.md) closes every issue with a closing keyword line, or marks it partial
    (`Refs #N (left: <what>)`); a note names the missing numbers (delivery adds their lines with
    --pr-body and checks the body with --closes)
  - docs rules (`docs.rules` in config.json, opt-in): a job whose diff touches a rule's `code`
    globs but none of its `docs` globs gets the issue `DOCS: missing update for <why> (<files>)`,
    which blocks auto-merge like any issue, until the docs are updated or the job's text (pr-body.md,
    decision.md, or round-N/build.md) has a line `Docs: not needed: <reason>`; a line
    that names a rule's `why` covers that rule only, one without covers every rule. An accepted
    line is a note `DOCS: not needed for <why>: <reason>`, and --pr-body quotes it in the PR/MR
Renames are diffed as delete + add, so a file moved to `.env` or into .factory/ is still seen.

Second line of the output: `AUTO-MERGE: yes` or `AUTO-MERGE: no: <reasons>` (with
`delivery.auto_merge_held: true` and no issues: `AUTO-MERGE: held: <reasons>`, which delivery
overrides; the reasons go into the PR/MR description). The mechanical half
of the auto-merge gate (.factory/job adds the rest): no issues, no audit deviation (⚠️; an
`⚠️ ENV:` row with a measured load or network cause, or a `⚠️ TEST:` row with a `verified by`
part, is not one), and no
file in a class that CI alone does not vouch for (CI/pipeline config, dependencies and
lockfiles, containers, schema and migrations, auth), plus whatever `delivery.auto_merge_never`
in config.json lists (globs; put personal-data paths there).
Also reported: files, lines, commits, [blocking] mentions per review (information only).
"""
